WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.368
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:03.703
and Golden Globe
award-winning actress

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:05.905
you know from her work
on "ER" and "The Good Wife."

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:09.575
Her memoir, "Sunshine Girl:
An Unexpected Life," is out now.

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:11.144
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:14.080
and the studio,
Julianna Margulies.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.916
♪♪

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:20.887
-Yay!
-Yay!

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.454
Yeah, I know.
-We can do that, right?

00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:23.823
We're gonna get there now.
-We can.

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:25.524
We're vaxxed.
-We're vaxxed.

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:26.793
-But I have to sit over here,
I guess.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:28.194
-Yeah, you got to be
in the middle of the couch.

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:29.195
-Hey.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:30.362
-Welcome.
-Wow.

00:00:30.362 --> 00:00:32.131
-What a delight
to have you here.

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:35.001
-It's -- I'm not
your first in-studio guest.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:36.669
-You're not
our first in-studio guest.

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:37.870
But I know you, obviously,

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.271
have been doing a lot of press
for this book.

00:00:39.271 --> 00:00:42.008
Have you been
in-studio anywhere else?

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:44.244
-I went in-studio --
I was in L.A. shooting

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.679
"The Morning Show"
and "The Kelly Clarkson Show,"

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:50.049
That was one of the first
in-person one.

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.684
It was strange,
because they did have people

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:53.586
in the seats,
but they were on screens.

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:55.788
-Yeah. They do like --
-That was odd.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.158
-So you could actually see
faces, but they were not --

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:59.392
-No.
-Not on bodies.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.360
-No, but most everything
is from my house.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.097
-All right.
-Which is kind of great, too...

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:05.831
-Yeah?
-...in that I'm not going to,

00:01:05.831 --> 00:01:08.101
you know, all the bookstores.
And, you know, I'm trying

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.602
to support all
the independent bookstores,

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.838
so I would be doing a tour...
-Of course.

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:15.040
-...that would take me
to strange motel rooms

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.743
and bad diners, so...
-I hope that those

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:19.745
virtual book tours
are still effective.

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:21.847
I would like to think
that people, you know,

00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:23.782
would log on to those Zooms and
still get to engage with you.

00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:25.584
-They're huge.
You're getting more people

00:01:25.584 --> 00:01:30.256
because anyone can log on,
so it's not just in that area.

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:32.825
So it's actually --
We actually just for --

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:34.527
The theater company
I'm on the board for

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:36.329
do this miscast every year.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:40.533
And we've had more people
being able to attend last night

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.468
and the year before
because of virtual --

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:44.270
because everyone
can buy a ticket.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:47.807
-Did you -- Do you do Q&amp;A's when
you do your virtual bookstore?

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:49.175
-Yeah.
-How are --

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:54.313
What percentage of the questions
are fully loony-bird?

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:55.949
-Most of them
are not about the book.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.884
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:01.687
Many of them are, "Are you ever
gonna do an 'ER' reunion?"

00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:03.422
-A lot of "ER" reunion
questions, sure.

00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:06.626
-Yeah, that kind of thing.
You know, "If someone fainted

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:08.461
on an airplane,
would you know what to do?"

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.096
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:11.397
-So, like, medical questions.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.698
People, a lot of
medical questions.

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:15.701
-Well, yeah, 'cause it's this
strange "ER" resurgence.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:19.271
I think, in quarantine, parents
must have said to their kids,

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:21.240
"There's nothing else to watch."
-Yep.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.410
-"I grew up watching 'ER.'
Let's re--," you know.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.145
-Well, there's so many
of those shows.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:29.081
-Yeah.
-Because it's sometimes daunting

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:30.583
when you look at a show
like "ER"

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:32.351
that has so many seasons...
-Yeah.

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:34.254
-...to start it again.
-Right. I think --

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:36.722
-Unless there's a pandemic, when
you've run out of everything.

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:37.857
And you're just like, "What?"
-Right. It's kind of --

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:41.527
Well, so,
we were in lockdown Upstate,

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:43.863
and my son at the time was 12.

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.832
And "The Office" became his...
-Yeah.

00:02:46.832 --> 00:02:49.302
-...religion.
I mean, we had to do

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:52.605
online questionnaires,
"Who's most like Pam?"

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.840
"Who's most like Jim?"
-Yep.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.310
-And I found out that most 12-,
13-, and 14-year-old boys --

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.346
we watched all nine seasons
of "The Office."

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:03.249
-Now, has he engaged with "ER"?

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.817
-My son? No.
-No interest?

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.420
-I tried.
-You did try, though.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.189
-I said, "You know,
I used to be on a show

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:13.092
that was pretty popular,
and you might --

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:14.226
you're old enough now
to see it,"

00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:16.662
and he was like, "I'm good."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:20.199
And I -- But he's been
like that since he was 3.

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:23.369
I did, for him --
-Yeah.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:27.239
-I did an episode
of "Sesame Street,"

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:29.808
where I play a doctor
fixing Big Bird's wing.

00:03:29.808 --> 00:03:30.843
There I am.
-Very well-cast.

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:32.077
Here's a photo
of you and Big Bird.

00:03:32.077 --> 00:03:34.147
There you go.
-And we set up the living room,

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.416
and he came out in his diaper
with his bottle.

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:39.752
He had just turned 3. And I
said, "Look, Kieran -- Mama!"

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.120
And he went, "Nope."

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.622
Stood up
and walked out of the room.

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.090
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.227
-I recently got to engage with
some of the Muppets, as well,

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:50.730
and it was right before
my kid's birthday.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.266
So at the end,
I took a video with me,

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:58.638
Elmo, and Cookie Monster,
and my 5-year-old loved it,

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:00.239
but then he said,
"Next time,

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:01.808
you don't have to be
in the video."

00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:03.209
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:04.977
'Cause you realize
it just takes them out of it.

00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:06.679
-Yeah, it takes them out of it.
-Yeah.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.014
-And I actually think
it's really healthy.

00:04:09.014 --> 00:04:11.116
I mean, he came
a couple of times to the set

00:04:11.116 --> 00:04:12.552
when I was doing
"The Good Wife."

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:15.455
And he was more interested in
sitting behind the camera and...

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:16.589
-Yeah.
-...putting the headphones on

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.424
and watching
from the director's chair.

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:20.660
He doesn't --
I'm his mom.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.161
-Yeah.
-That's how he wants to see me.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:23.796
And I think that's fine.
-Right.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.364
-But he won't
be reading the book.

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:27.733
-Yeah. Well, I do think
there's that funny thing

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:31.704
of when you know what your mom
does for a living,

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:32.905
and you know
what she's capable of,

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.106
and you can't watch a show
where you're like,

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.508
"She's not a nurse."
-Right.

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:37.310
And he said that.
He was like, "It's just gonna

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.478
make me embarrassed, Mom,
because I know that's not you."

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:40.913
-Yeah.
-And I was like, "Okay.

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.081
I get it."
-That's -- yeah.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.150
-You know,
maybe when I'm dead and gone,

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.286
he'll get in his rocker and go,
"Oh, that was my mother."

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:49.155
-You mentioned
"The Morning Show,"

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:54.393
and I know you can't speak much
to the plot or who you play.

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:56.895
But I would imagine, you know,
this is a set where there

00:04:56.895 --> 00:05:00.733
are a lot of people who have
worked on long-running TV shows.

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:03.102
I would assume they're all
bringing what they've learned,

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:05.371
and it's a pretty
nice environment.

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.307
-It was a little
slice of heaven for me.

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:09.575
I mean, I felt
like I was on a vacation.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:12.911
Because to go from lockdown,
you know,

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:15.314
with your immediate family
for eight months,

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:18.584
where you're cooking, cleaning,
shopping, ironing,

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:20.686
doing all of that stuff,
to suddenly being on the set

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.655
with these incredible actors.

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:26.459
And also, for me, I mean,
it was kind of a joke

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:30.629
because it was like, "So, on
Tuesday, you're in one scene."

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:34.067
And I was like, "I get a week
to learn one scene?"

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:36.169
'Cause I'm so used
to having to learn

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:37.736
nine pages of dialogue a day.
-Sure.

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:40.006
-And, you know, I can't learn it
until that night

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:43.075
when I get the dialogue I know
for that day out of my head.

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:45.178
So it was one of those things
where I was like,

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:47.046
"This is great."

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:49.248
I mean I was in heaven.
It was really fun.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:54.186
And all I can say is,
that my character is a --

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:59.225
a news anchor, journalist,
very successful in her world.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:02.762
She has a one-hour news program
once a week at night.

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:05.664
And that's all,
apparently, I can say.

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.966
-That's, like, meaningless,
what you just gave us.

00:06:07.966 --> 00:06:09.435
-It's meaningless, I know.

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:11.237
[ Laughter ]
I'm sorry.

00:06:11.237 --> 00:06:15.308
They actually -- Apple sent out
a whole thing of my answers.

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:18.710
And I realized I had spoken
to a few newspapers beforehand

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.646
and said some things.

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:21.947
-Oh, that you weren't
allowed to say anything?

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:23.215
-That apparently
I wasn't supposed to say...

00:06:23.215 --> 00:06:26.785
-Did the Apple police...
-I'm trying to be good now.

00:06:26.785 --> 00:06:28.521
-Here's something I found out
from the book

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:30.223
that I cannot believe
I did not know,

00:06:30.223 --> 00:06:33.159
that you lived in New Hampshire.

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:37.696
I feel like I have a pretty
good grasp of other people

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:38.864
who've lived in New Hampshire,

00:06:38.864 --> 00:06:40.933
as someone
who also lived in New Hampshire.

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:42.668
I did not know this about you.
-There's only a few of us.

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:44.870
-There's not that many.
-I know, yeah.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.205
-Did you have good --
Were your experiences

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:47.540
in New Hampshire positive ones?

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.009
-So, yes, some of them --
most of them.

00:06:51.009 --> 00:06:54.112
But I got there in eighth grade,
and I had come straight from

00:06:54.112 --> 00:06:56.782
living in England,
where it just --

00:06:56.782 --> 00:06:59.385
I was always
trying to figure out

00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:01.153
who I was supposed to be now.
-Yeah.

00:07:01.153 --> 00:07:02.387
-Because everything
was different.

00:07:02.387 --> 00:07:03.923
And when I got there, it was --

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:06.759
You know, like, I showed up
in what I had been, you know,

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:08.861
thinking was so chic
and awesome,

00:07:08.861 --> 00:07:10.530
which were these big red
kicker shoes.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.097
They were called kicker --
And FU's jeans,

00:07:12.097 --> 00:07:13.932
and I showed up
into my classroom,

00:07:13.932 --> 00:07:17.703
and they're all in Timberland
boots and flannel shirts.

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:21.240
And I remember one kid
saw my outfit and was like,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.910
"Jeezum Crow."

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.912
And I didn't know what that was.

00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:27.313
Like, was that a good thing?
-Right.

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:29.114
-A bad thing?

00:07:29.114 --> 00:07:31.117
Turns out it means
"Jesus Christ."

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.385
-Yep.
-Jeezum Crow.

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:36.121
So when I ran home,

00:07:36.121 --> 00:07:38.224
we were living in
my gym teacher's house.

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:39.959
Long story, read the book.
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:44.130
-But he lived on campus,
and I ran home to change,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.532
because I was -- at lunchtime --
because I was so embarrassed,

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:48.567
and I came back for Latin.

00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:51.370
And my Latin teacher,
I hadn't met her yet.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.106
Her name was Mrs. "Lombahdi".

00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:56.108
Which really translate
to Mrs. Lombardi.

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:58.710
-Yeah, we had a Mrs. Lombardi
in my school, too.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.947
-Did you?
-Yep.

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:04.617
-I walked in and she said,
"You're tardy."

00:08:04.617 --> 00:08:07.920
And I had a slight British
accent, so I said,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.823
"I'm so sorry.
What does that mean?"

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:14.427
And she thought
I was being cheeky, you know,

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:16.995
by not knowing what
"You're tardy" means.

00:08:16.995 --> 00:08:20.532
And so it went back and forth --
"T-A-R-D-Y!"

00:08:20.532 --> 00:08:22.834
[ Laughter ]
And I was like, "I'm tardy.

00:08:22.834 --> 00:08:24.937
I'm tardy."
And finally a kid was like,

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:28.140
"You're late, you're tardy."
"Oh, my God, thank you!"

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:31.244
I felt like I moved from London
and moved to a different country

00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:32.978
speaking a different language.
-Yeah.

00:08:32.978 --> 00:08:34.647
No, it turns out --

00:08:34.647 --> 00:08:37.483
Do not mistake New England
for England.

00:08:37.483 --> 00:08:41.954
-No! No. My mom hired
a handyman to help us.

00:08:41.954 --> 00:08:43.856
It was my mom
and me and my sister.

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:46.025
And when we finally moved in
to our house,

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:49.094
I just wanted to hang up my,
like, curtains and bookshelves

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:51.530
and have a room of my own.
And he kept --

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:52.797
I wanted to help him,
and he kept saying,

00:08:52.797 --> 00:08:55.067
"Just pot it on the mok."

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:57.769
And I was like, "I will.
What does that mean?"

00:08:57.769 --> 00:08:58.938
"Pot it on the mok."

00:08:58.938 --> 00:09:02.307
"Put it on the mark"
is what it mean.

00:09:02.307 --> 00:09:03.742
I would just -- I was like,
"Oh, my God.

00:09:03.742 --> 00:09:05.578
I'm never gonna win."
So it was --

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:06.779
Yeah, that's New Hampshire.

00:09:06.779 --> 00:09:08.114
Why don't you have
a New Hampshire accent?

00:09:08.114 --> 00:09:11.283
-I -- You know, I grew up --
I was in Michigan first.

00:09:11.283 --> 00:09:12.751
And then,
by the time I got there,

00:09:12.751 --> 00:09:15.187
it was a little late to break.
It took me a while.

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:19.659
I had some like -- I certainly
said "wicked" a great amount.

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:21.593
And that I sort of
slowly combed out.

00:09:21.593 --> 00:09:22.795
But it's hard
because my mom --

00:09:22.795 --> 00:09:25.798
-"Wicked" is classic.
-My mom still says it.

00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:29.267
Because my mom was then
a schoolteacher, and so, like,

00:09:29.267 --> 00:09:32.271
she just was constantly around,
like, whatever slang

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:34.873
that New Hampshire kids
of the day used.

00:09:34.873 --> 00:09:36.274
-But you never said,
"Jeezum Crow."

00:09:36.274 --> 00:09:38.544
-I never said, "Jeezum Crow."
But I will say,

00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:39.879
based on the outfit
you described,

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:41.881
if I'd seen that,
I would have said it.

00:09:41.881 --> 00:09:45.517
-I mean -- I mean, cut to,
you know, six months later.

00:09:45.517 --> 00:09:47.920
I, too -- Well, I never went
the Timberland boot thing,

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.755
but I had
the L.L.Bean boots on...

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:51.023
-Yeah.
-...and carpenter pants.

00:09:51.023 --> 00:09:52.324
Like, that's as far
as I would go.

00:09:52.324 --> 00:09:53.826
I think I had a couple
flannel shirts.

00:09:53.826 --> 00:09:57.930
-Yeah, well, we do appreciate
that you at least went that far.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:59.898
-Yeah, I did.
And then I loved it.

00:09:59.898 --> 00:10:01.066
I actually loved
my high school years there.

00:10:01.066 --> 00:10:03.436
-It's a really --
It's a great place.

00:10:03.436 --> 00:10:04.704
-Yeah.
And we'd ski.

00:10:04.704 --> 00:10:06.605
They shut down schools
on Wednesday,

00:10:06.605 --> 00:10:08.607
and we all went skiing
because there was too much snow.

00:10:08.607 --> 00:10:11.043
-Yeah. I mean, that --
The amount of snow days

00:10:11.043 --> 00:10:12.545
I had growing up...
-Yeah, it was great.

00:10:12.545 --> 00:10:15.381
-I mean, I wonder with climate
change if there are as many,

00:10:15.381 --> 00:10:18.049
but I felt like --
-Haven't you heard the latest?

00:10:18.049 --> 00:10:19.885
My son is furious.
-What's up?

00:10:19.885 --> 00:10:22.087
-There's no more snow days
because of Zoom.

00:10:22.087 --> 00:10:24.556
-Oh, yeah.
-Now all kids can go on Zoom

00:10:24.556 --> 00:10:26.892
and they don't need to
miss school.

00:10:26.892 --> 00:10:28.861
-There would need to be
a citywide blackout.

00:10:28.861 --> 00:10:30.762
-That's right.
-That's the new snow day.

00:10:30.762 --> 00:10:33.665
-Technical glitch.
-Hey, thank you for being here.

00:10:33.665 --> 00:10:34.900
Thank you for being here
in studio.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:36.635
It's always such a delight
to see you.

00:10:36.635 --> 00:10:38.571
-A pleasure.
Thanks for having me.

00:10:38.571 --> 00:10:41.340
-"Sunshine Girl" is available
now wherever books are sold.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.108
But please support your local
and independent bookstores.

00:10:43.108 --> 00:10:44.276
We'll be right back.

